Now I understand the vulnerability. Let me analyze `lex_multiline_string`:

```c
static void *lex_multiline_string(struct lexer *lexer)
{
    char *end = (peek(lexer) == '"') ? "\"\"\"" : "'''";

    advance_n(lexer, strlen("'''") - 1);

    do {
        if (!strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)) {
            emit(lexer, LEXEME_STRING);
            lexer->pos += 3;

            return lex_config;
        }
    } while (next(lexer) != '\0');

    return lex_error(lexer, "EOF while scanning multiline string");
}
```

The bug: `strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)` reads 3 bytes from `lexer->pos` without checking if `lexer->pos` is within bounds. If the input ends with `'''` or `"""` at the very end of the buffer, or if the multiline string is never terminated, `lexer->pos` can point past the end of the buffer, causing a global-buffer-overflow READ.

